Add DeltaChip — one component for every "versus last period"

The most repeated element in the eight reference cards, and the thing the
critic found missing: every "up or down" in this product was a `Badge` — a 4px
box with uppercase monospace, parked in the card header, decoupled from the
number it was describing. A reader scanning the header learned the movement
before learning the figure. That is refusal #13.

The chip is a tinted pill on the hero number's own baseline, optically centred
on it rather than box-centred. Direction selects `--ns-money-pos` / `-neg` /
`-flat` and the tint is a `color-mix` of the same token, so dark mode re-tints
without a second rule (T3).

`absent` is the case that matters most and is the easiest to lose: a run with
no prior period has no movement, and a movement that does not exist is not a
0.0% one. It renders the em dash in `--ns-absent` (M1) with an sr-only "no
prior period" — the same discipline the money layer applies to a null amount.

The chip never formats and never infers: `value` arrives already signed and
already formatted (H2), so `+2.1%` and `-$1,204.00` are both just strings.

Registered in `ledgerlineCatalog` with a demo covering all five states —
up, down, flat, the glyph-less hero variant the reference's headline chip
uses, and absent — plus the trailing note form.
EOF
