Add SegmentedControl — range selection by filling, not by bordering

The critic's ranked item 6, first half. The reference's range selector —
`1D 1W 1M 3M 1Y` — appears in six of the eight reference cards and did not
exist here at all. The only thing the product could offer was a `<Select>`,
which is a different affordance: a range is a projection over data already on
the page, not a navigation (D5).

The selected cell is a filled pill, and that is the whole rule (C4). A selected
state that only changes a border is rejected, because a border change reads as
hover. The selected pill takes the surface's own background rather than a
theme-specific colour, so light and dark both read without a second rule (T3).

Two details that are easy to get wrong and both show up here:

· Dividers are drawn on the leading edge of every cell but the first, so they
  never double up and never sit on the capsule's own edge. A selected cell
  swallows the divider that would run into it.
· Link options turn the whole control into navigation and drop the tab
  semantics, because a link is not a tab — selection is then announced with
  `aria-current`. Button and link options are not mixed; the first option
  decides the mode for the whole control.

`aria-selected` is written as an explicit "true"/"false" rather than a
boolean: an empty `aria-selected` reads as false to assistive tech, which
would leave the control with no selected tab at all.

Registered in `ledgerlineCatalog` with three demos — the full-width collection
period, the compact run window with a disabled cell, and the link form — since
the disabled state is a state the gallery has to be seen wearing (D4).
EOF
