Add ComparisonAreaChart — billed versus collected as two layers

The critic's ranked item 5, second half. There was no area chart anywhere in
the product: no SVG area geometry, and no way to render a translucent
comparison overlay. The existing `ChartBlock` is HTML `<div>` columns and
`<span>` bars sized with inline percentages, and it formats its own tick labels
from floats — which is H1 and H2 both broken at once.

This one is hand-authored SVG (H1) and formats nothing: `value` is geometry
only, and every label the reader sees arrives preformatted as a string (H2).

The comparison period is the *same hue* at reduced opacity, not a second
colour and not a second stroke (H5, H9). Two layers told apart by hue force a
legend lookup; two layers told apart by weight do not. There is never a second
axis.

Hover is CSS (H4). The crosshair, the point marker and the dark pointer bubble
reveal on `:hover` of a full-height column per point, so the reader aims at an
x rather than at a 4px dot, and there is no hydration cost and no first-paint
mismatch. The same facts are emitted as sr-only text, because a CSS-only
readout is invisible to a screen reader.

Chart chrome stays minimal (H6): one hairline frame, four faint dashed
gridlines, and axis labels sitting *outside* the bounded plot. The chart
exports `areaChartMax` and `AREA_CHART_TICK_RATIOS` so a caller builds its tick
labels from the same maximum the gridlines sit on — a tick that disagrees with
its own gridline is a lie about the scale. Verified: gridlines land at
y = 10 / 37.5 / 65 / 92.5 and the tick tops at 8.33 / 31.25 / 54.17 / 77.08 /
100%, which are exactly those y values as a fraction of the 120-unit plot.

No data renders a labelled dashed frame, never a flat line at zero (H7).
"Nothing recorded in this period" and "the value was zero" are different
statements.

The gallery fixtures land with it: twelve months of one billing book, with the
four ranges derived from the same twelve months rather than typed by hand, so
the ranges cannot disagree with each other. Fixture arithmetic is exact
BigInt decimal-string maths, because a fixture that drifts by a cent produces a
demo whose footer does not add up. They live in `(_shared)` and do not import
from an island — the server half of the page should not pull in a client module
to read a type.
EOF
