Add FooterColumns — hairline rules, not gaps

The critic's ranked item 6, second half, and the band that closes the card.
The reference's cards end in a strip of equal columns — approved / excepted /
excluded, or invoiced / attempted / collected — and `Card.Footer` here was a
grey padded bar: no partitions, no columns, no way to read three related facts
as one strip.

Columns are equal-width and divided by hairline *rules*, never by gaps (C5): a
gap reads as two separate facts, a rule reads as one strip of related facts.
The divider is drawn on the leading edge of every column but the first, so it
never doubles up and never sits on the strip's own outer edge.

Direction is a domain fact the call site states, not a colour it picks (M5):
approved is towards us, excepted is away. Omitted leaves the amount in the
surface's own foreground.

A `null` column renders the em dash in the absent tone, and this is exactly
where a fabricated `0.00` would do the most damage — "no wire payments
recorded" and "zero paid by wire" are different statements about money (M1).
Verified in the render: the null column emits `—` plus an sr-only "not
recorded", never a zero.

With this the five bands are complete, so the gallery now proves the
composition rather than just cataloguing the parts: `RangeControlDemo` is a
single `WidgetCard` whose segmented control drives the area chart, the hero
amount and its delta chip, over a hairline footer — a working period selector
over data already on the page (I2, D5). It is the only island among the five,
and the only thing it owns is the selected range.

The gauge and widget-card demos are upgraded to close with real footer columns
instead of a CTA, so every composed card in the gallery is now wearing the full
grammar.
EOF
