[0m$ [0mcd /home/agent/projects/netscript/wave7-billing-steer2 && git add -A apps/ledgerline-web/assets && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'
Give the destructive button's hover a role instead of a ramp step

Found by porting the contract test, not by reading. `button.css` hovered to
`var(--ns-rust-6)`, and the same rule has to work on two grounds.

rust-6 is 54.5% lightness. Against a light card that is a barely-perceptible
step from rust-5, so the hover does not read as a hover at all; against a dark
card it fails contrast outright. It is the same defect the critic found in
`exception-badge.css` and that T2a exists to prevent — a component reaching
past the semantic layer into a ramp whose meaning inverts.

The hover pair is a *role*, because the direction it should step depends on the
ground: darker on paper, lighter on ink. `--ns-destructive-hover` is added to
the token layer as rust-7 in light and rust-4 in dark, matching the
`-hover` role the primary and secondary variants already use, and the component
now names the role.

Token parity held and re-checked: 189 declared in JSON, 189 in CSS, no
divergence either way.
