Give the product tree the design contract test

Every rule in DESIGN.md that is mechanically checkable now actually runs here.
The design tree has had this file since the steer's first pass; the product
tree never did, which is the difference between "these defects are fixed" and
"these defects stay fixed".

Thirteen tests, and three of them found real work the moment they ran:

· T2a caught `button.css` hovering to `--ns-rust-6` — a ramp step, so the
  hover is imperceptible on paper and fails contrast on a dark card. Fixed in
  the previous commit with a `--ns-destructive-hover` role.
· R8 caught the scaffold fiction the critic flagged, plus a lot more it did not
  name, including `netscript service:add` in an empty state. Fixed in the
  previous commit.
· M8 scans `components/product/` as well as `components/ui/`, because that is
  where the money defect actually lived. The path had to be adapted from the
  design tree's copy: MoneyText is a product component here, not a UI kit one.

What is now guarded: no component reaches for a ramp step (T2a), no raw colour
literal in a stylesheet (T2), no offset shadow (C8), only `lib/money` turns a
decimal into text (M8), tokens.json and tokens.css never diverge and every
token a component uses is declared (T1), every stylesheet is imported (T6),
every module is exported from the barrel (R1), every Ledgerline catalogue entry
has a gallery demo and the upstream count is not inflated by app-owned widgets
(R3), gauge arcs terminate with a flat cut (H8), and no scaffold fiction ships
in any app source (R8).

The dead-link check also guards against itself going vacuous: it fails if it
finds no href literals at all, so a rewrite that moves every href into a prop
cannot leave it passing while proving nothing.
EOF
